<h2>Build the export and dependency packet first</h2>
<p>Inventory every automation, template, dynamic product block, form, webhook, theme snippet, checkout extension, API caller and report. For each, record its owner, source event, IDs, destination and downstream consumers. Include subscription, loyalty, review and helpdesk connections; removing a marketing app can interrupt work outside marketing. Screenshot editable logic and save an original export with field definitions, time zones and date.</p>
<p>Reconcile active, suppressed, unsubscribed and deleted states separately. Preserve permission source, timestamp, purpose and channel, not just contact addresses. Do not merge guest checkouts by address alone or let an older import overwrite a newer opt-out. Ask support early about unavailable exports and retention; record gaps instead of inventing missing evidence. The responsible reviewer sets permission and retention rules for the actual audience.</p>
<h2>Trial the requirement, not the sales demonstration</h2>
<p>For a proposed skincare billing review, inspect giveaway imports and billing eligibility, then test a serum and moisturizer with different replenishment intervals and a mixed basket. Keep subscription customers out where the store’s policy requires it. Compare staying with corrected hygiene against candidate quotes and actual operator effort. No merchant results, 21-day delivery promise or labor reduction is claimed.</p>
<p>Separate an anonymous cart, identified checkout and completed order. Replay a stable event key, introduce a late purchase, withdraw permission after enrollment and change stock before dispatch. Require the intended enrollment, deduplication, exit and message destination for each fixture. Test mobile layout, dark mode, discount codes, product links, unsubscribe and preference center. Keep screenshots and IDs as acceptance evidence; these exercises have not been executed in supplier accounts for this guide.</p>
<h2>Cut over by queue ownership</h2>
<p>Assign one sender to each purpose before activation. List delayed work in both systems, freeze edits for handoff, cancel or suppress the previous owner and verify cancellation actually removes queued work. Recheck purchase, permission, frequency and eligibility at send time. Coordinate SMS and email exclusions explicitly rather than assuming shared suppression between separate apps.</p>
<p>Inventory transactional notices independently: order and shipping notifications should keep their documented sender and calling integration until an accepted change is ready. Test reply routing, open support conversations, SMS opt-out handling and escalation after hours. A campaign migration is incomplete if customers can receive messages but replies have no owner.</p>
<p>Set timing from queue delays, export access, number transfer and operating coverage. Agree any email delivery ramp with the receiving provider using volume, reputation and engagement; expand from observed checks rather than a fixed calendar or automatic VIP broadcast. Keep a named kill-switch owner available during the actual handoff.</p>
<h2>Budget the full operating change</h2>
<p>Use dated quotes for identical audience and send assumptions. Include retained capture, SMS, notification or support services, message charges, implementation, agency work, operator time and overlap. Price staying with hygiene, a narrow replacement and a wider change. Neither a subscription saving nor an attributed revenue increase alone proves incremental profit.</p>
<p>Illustrative math: 60 hours × $75 loaded hourly cost = $4,500. An $8,000 total project with $200 monthly net fee savings takes 40 months to repay on fees alone. If a separately justified scenario yields $1,800 monthly incremental contribution after variable costs, $8,000 ÷ $1,800 is about 4.44 months before any additional fixed costs. These are hypothetical inputs, not supplier prices or promised lift. Model zero lift and higher maintenance as well.</p>
<p>SMS comparisons need destination, carrier fees, number costs and message counts; push needs supported subscriptions and send allowances. Compare contribution after discounts, returns and fulfillment, deduplicate orders across channels and preserve attribution-window definitions and UTM naming. Do not compare a new attribution window with an old one as if the difference were causal growth.</p>
<h2>Keep rollback and ownership usable</h2>
<p>Save configurations and a record of already-sent and pending IDs. Rehearse pausing a faulty replacement, reconciling queue state and restoring only eligible unsent work to the previous owner if still supported. Retired services may offer no live rollback: document that constraint and prepare a controlled pause and manual support route instead. Do not cancel data access while unresolved records or support cases still depend on it.</p>
<p>Assign copy, discount, suppression, capture, reporting, replies and billing owners. Define agency rebuild and training scope, approval boundaries and contractor access expiry. Train a second operator. Review delivery, complaints, opt-outs, duplicate incidents, maintenance effort and contribution against the saved baseline; use observed problems to decide what to change next. Retire unused credentials and confirm final invoices once retention and rollback needs are resolved.</p>
<h2>Choose the next reading step</h2>
<p>Use the incumbent guide for export and continuity questions, a comparison when two candidates remain, and a use-case playbook for the workflow fixture. A guide link is a starting point; verify current supplier scope and quotes before committing. No candidate, including Sequenzy, receives automatic first place.</p>
